title start
kind: default
tags: notation book plan
Plan: books, sciences, skills and reading mode
title end

section start
title start
Section 1: the idea
title end

paragraph start
One source, many uses. A page is written once and then read as a book, run as a checklist, kept as rules, followed as a routine or practiced as memorization cards. Writing is only one use of a page.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Everything works offline. Every file and directory is readable in the terminal with the same structure and automation as today. Nothing is stuck online. Printing and export come last, only after everything else is finished and proven.
paragraph end

paragraph start
A field (a law, a skill, a science) is built from many files in many directories joined by connections. Example: driving school leads to driving itself, which needs the driving laws and the driving skills, and connects to mechanics and off-roading. From such a field one complete checklist, one book or one practice set is compiled.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Everything stays computed from structure: numbers, contents, index, cross references, page numbers, checklists. None of it is stored by hand in the page.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 2: vocabulary
title end

paragraph start
chapter replaces section, and title is the only heading: heading blocks are removed. Chapters hold chapters. Every other name on the book side is renamed to match. The number is computed from nesting, never written.
paragraph end

paragraph start
The book parts are block types, each one a .blocktype file: cover, title-page, copyright, preface, contents, chapter, figure, table, footnote, reference, bibliography, index, volume.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 3: two modes over the same source
title end

paragraph start
Writing mode: every detail and every technical part is visible: declarations, contracts, questions, answers, todos, comments, names, targets, marks. This is the editor as it is now.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Reading mode: the technical parts are hidden. The text is properly formatted like a presentation, with numbers, chapters, tables and figures drawn in their final form. An entire file, book or science can be read linearly and fast, in a mind map style that unfolds. No technical question is shown in reading mode.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Both modes work on the same file. Switching never changes the file. Everything stays keyboard driven.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 4: marks on the sides
title end

paragraph start
The author marks a block as a rule, a step, a fact, a routine or a card. Marking is explicit, so what a checklist contains is predictable and checkable.
paragraph end

paragraph start
In writing mode a mark is drawn on the side of the block: a check box for a step that belongs to a checklist, a rule sign on the other side for a rule. In reading mode the marks are hidden.
paragraph end

paragraph start
A block can carry more than one mark. One block can feed several views.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 5: views compiled from marks
title end

paragraph start
Checklist: the steps of a field in order, across files. Rules: every rule of a field in one list. Routines: steps that repeat. Practice: facts and cards for memorization. Book: chapters in order with generated parts.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Progress (what is ticked, what was practiced, when) is not content. It is stored in its own file or in the database, never in the page, and it points to a block by its permanent name.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 6: connections and the database
title end

paragraph start
Connections between files use the existing name: and target: of blocks. The notation database holds the connections so a compiled view can pull from any directory. The files stay the truth: the database can always be rebuilt from them.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Subdirectories and files for a field are created by the system so the structure stays consistent. This builds on the notation database and the ecosystem server already planned.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 7: order of work
title end

paragraph start
Step 1: remove heading, rename section to chapter, number chapters by nesting and show the number on every chapter title, also on flat pages. Rust and Racket numbering stay in agreement.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Step 2: load block types per file (already a todo), then the book parts and the marked block types: rule, step, fact, routine, card.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Step 3: writing mode and reading mode, with marks on the sides in writing mode only.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Step 4: connections and the database for fields.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Step 5: compiled views: checklist, rules, routines, practice, book with contents, index, bibliography and cross references.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Step 6: edition: page size, margins, headers, footers, page numbers that belong to one edition.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Step 7 (last, only when everything above is proven): export and printing.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Rule of work: before each step look for holes and amplifiers, and every time something is updated it is written into these files at once.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 8: holes to close before implementing
title end

paragraph start
Decided, rename: there is no heading block any more. Only title is used. No existing page uses heading start, so nothing is migrated. The heading blocktype file and the heading code in the parser and in numbering.rs are removed.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, chapter: chapter replaces section. A chapter holds its own title, text and chapters inside it. The number is computed from how deep the chapter sits and is shown on its title. Hole left: numbering.rs numbers heading blocks by level, so it is reworked to number chapters by nesting, in Rust and Racket together.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, marks: a mark is a block of its own. It sits inside the block it marks, like answer, or wraps several blocks, like comment. Both forms are allowed. The names are decided in section 10.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, reading mode: everything technical that is not part of reading the information is hidden. Visible: titles, chapters, text, code, tables, figures, footnotes, nothing else. The exact list is in section 11.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Progress identity: if a block is renamed or moved, its progress must follow. Needs a permanent id, not only the name.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Moved or deleted files: connections to them must be reported by the check, not silently broken.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Order across files: a checklist over several files needs an order. Decide how prerequisites are written (needs and leads-to) and what happens on a cycle.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Conflicting rules: two laws can disagree, or change by place and date. Decide whether a rule carries a scope.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Terminal width: reading mode must wrap to the screen width, with no line past it.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Navigation: how to jump between chapters, back to the mind map level, and to a connected file while reading.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Database rebuild: how long a full rebuild takes on the phone and when it runs.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, marks in links: marks are counted only in the original block, never in a shown copy. A routine file only refers to blocks, so it never makes a second step.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 9: amplifiers to consider
title end

paragraph start
Learning path: prerequisites give the order in which a skill or science is learned, for example driving school before driving.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Spaced practice: cards are scheduled by when they were last practiced. Only the schedule is stored, in the progress file.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Coverage report: which rules have no checklist step, which facts have no card, which chapters have no mark at all.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Exam mode: reading mode with the answers hidden, for self testing.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Glossary: built from the facts and the index terms of a field.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Field templates: a starting set of files for a law, a skill or a science, created by one command.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Mind map view of the connections of a field, read in the terminal.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Reading bookmark: reading mode remembers where you stopped, stored as progress.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Routines with times: a routine step can carry how often it repeats.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 10: mark blocks and routines
title end

paragraph start
The mark blocks are rule, step, fact, routine and card. Each one is a block of its own and each one is a .blocktype file. Marks sit inside a block or wrap several blocks.
paragraph end

paragraph start
A routine is a wrapper. It wraps step blocks, because a routine is made of steps.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Making a routine creates a file for it, to learn or practice something. The file refers to the marked blocks in the original files by their names. Its numbering is automatic. The order is changed by moving the blocks in that file, and the original files are not changed.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Routine mode: starting a routine walks through the original files and shows the specific part of each step, one after the other.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Pause: the routine can be paused at any step and the file can be read as normal, so the reader can look around in the book and then go on. The place where it stopped is progress, kept in the progress file and not in the page.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Modular: a block can be in several routines, a routine file can be moved or copied alone, and the original files never know which routines use them.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Holes left: the name of the routine mode and its keys. What the check reports when a routine step points at a block that was moved or deleted. How the routine file is written, one reference block per step. Whether a routine can hold another routine.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 11: what reading mode shows and hides
title end

paragraph start
Visible in reading mode: the blocks title, chapter, paragraph, code, table, header, row, and later figure and footnote. The declarations that change how the text reads: language, caption, column, cell, format, style, font, theme, color.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Hidden in reading mode: the blocks comment, answer and every mark block (rule, step, fact, routine, card). The declarations tags, kind, for, asked, name, todo, meta, row-id and level. A todo line is hidden as a line, never the whole block. The target line is hidden too, but the link it makes stays visible, see section 12.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Questions and contracts exist so the author is reminded again and again of the context, the rule or the reminder that belongs to a kind of block, for example the questions of a title. There are many of them, everywhere, so in writing mode they are shown shrunk, not in full, and opened on demand. In reading mode they are hidden.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Holes left: confirm that meta and format are not hidden by accident. How a shrunk question looks and how it is opened. Exam mode may show answers on demand, kept as an amplifier.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 12: links
title end

paragraph start
A link is always visible, in reading mode and in writing mode, so the reader sees at all times where it goes to and where it comes from. The sign of a link is its target written in parentheses after the linked text. The editor draws the parentheses by itself, they are never typed and never stored.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Example: there is a driving school book and a driving book. In the driving book a link block holds the face "Once you finish driving school" and the target, and the page shows the text followed by the target in parentheses. In reading mode it shows where it goes to. In writing mode the block can be changed.
paragraph end

paragraph start
This needs more thought before anything is built. The aim is a really good linking system that shows to where and from where.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, link kind: the kind of a link (a plain reference or a prerequisite) is written as a declaration on the block, so the text stays clean to read.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, backlinks: the places a block is linked from are shown next to the block itself, never in a separate list or place. They are computed from the database, never written by hand.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, jump trail: following a link marks the spot you left. A back key returns to that spot. If you jump several times, the trail keeps every spot, and two keys exist: back goes one jump at a time, and original goes straight to the place where the trail started, however many jumps were made. The trail is progress, kept outside the page.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, link writing: a link is written as a block, link start and link end, like every other block. Completion in the editor, like code completion, offers the existing names while the link is typed. When one is chosen, the editor closes the block with link end. The file stores only the target name. The editor draws the target in parentheses after the text, for example: bla bla (target). The block is the stored form and the parentheses are only drawn.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, link block: a link block holds two declarations. face: is the text the reader sees, and target: is the name of the block or file it points to. Example: link start, face: Once you finish driving school, target: driving-school, link end. The page shows the face followed by the target in parentheses. face is a new declaration keyword and must be registered in model/properties.rkt when this is built. The earlier needs: declaration is deleted because there is no clear need for it yet.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, link destination: the parentheses always show the full target: the book, the computed chapter number and the title, for example (Driving school book: 3.2 Getting started). It is computed from the target, never stored, so it stays correct when chapters move. A link with no face: shows the full target as its text as well, so face: is optional. A target that is gone shows as broken, with its stored name and the word missing, and the check reports it.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Holes left: whether a link can be a prerequisite in a checklist across files, decided when compiled checklists are built. How a link to a whole book differs from a link to one chapter or one step. How long the trail is kept and whether it survives closing the editor. Whether buffer.rs holds a cursor position the trail can reuse.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Section 13: files, dirs and introductions
title end

paragraph start
Decided, naming: a file is called a file and a dir is called a dir. Book is no longer a kind of dir. Book is the way things are written inside files: chapters, computed numbering, reading mode, marks and links. There is no shelf and no master book as a kind.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, introduction: every dir has an introduction file that controls it, because a dir holds many files. Making something with the o key creates the dir and its introduction together, automatically. Dirs without an introduction are reported by the check.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, master: the introductions are listed upward. The introduction of a dir lists the introductions of the dirs inside it, for example universe, galaxy, planets, earth. The top introduction is the master of all. When something is made, the author chooses where it goes: under an existing entry, or beside it in the same category. The new introduction is added to its parent list automatically.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, reading: reading a dir in reading mode walks through its introduction, then its files and dirs in order. A link shows the full path in the parentheses and stores only the name, so moving or renaming never breaks a link.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, bundle: a bundle groups several files or dirs on the same subject, for example driving law, driving skill and driving school. It is a file with an ordered list of links, so the order gives the learning path.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, order: files and dirs are never numbered by hand and their names carry no numbers. The order is held by the introduction of each dir as an ordered list of its children. Numbers are computed from position. The order is changed in a tree view, like a tree file manager, by moving an entry up, down, in or out, and the tree view rewrites the lists. The lists stay plain text, so the files stay the truth.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, no lost file: in the tree view nothing can be lost, every file and dir has a place. The master introduction holds all categories and each category holds its own subcategories, so the whole system opens as one mind map style view. The database indexes this tree and is rebuilt from the introductions.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, tree view: the tree view is a fork of broot, 1.55.0 is installed, with its source changed directly, the same way the shell was forked, instead of configuring it. Every move, create and rename goes through the ecosystem and the database, so nothing is ever outside it. This is a later step: it comes only after the writing system works, because writing the files comes first.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Decided, order of work: first the writing system (chapter rename, numbering, marks, links, reading mode), then the tree view fork. Until then dirs and files are made by hand.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Holes left: how a move changes the dir on disk and both parent lists without a half done state. A file or dir on disk that is not in its parent list is proposed to show in an unplaced group and be reported by the check. Whether a bundle can hold other bundles. Rename the word book in sections 1 to 12 and the existing book file type to the new words. Whether the old heading todo wording needs changing.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Remaining, numbering: json.rkt still writes heading. Remove heading everywhere: heading blocktype, parser level checks and bad-heading-level, rich.rs arm, aiu-text.rkt, targets.rkt, security, numbering.test. Rename section to chapter in 13 code files and 12 pages. Record a numbering test.
paragraph end
section end
